=begin pod

=head1 NAME

B<App::RakuDocToPDF> - Provides routines to convert RakuDoc to PDF

See an example at L<click here | ./examples/pdf/README.pdf>
=head1 SYNOPSIS

From the command line:

=begin code

rakudoc2pdf README.rakudoc

rakudoc2pdf README.rakudoc --output=README.pdf

rakudoc2pdf README.rakudoc --media=A4
rakudoc2pdf README.rakudoc --type=module-readme

=end code

From Raku:

=begin code :lang<raku>
use App::RakuDocToPDF;

my IO::Path $pdf = rakudoc-to-pdf(
'README.rakudoc',
:output<README.pdf>,
:media<Letter>,
:type<module-readme>,
);
=end code

=head1 DESCRIPTION

B<App::RakuDocToPDF> converts simple RakuDoc files to paginated PDF documents.

Its primary purpose is to produce a printable draft of a module's
RakuDoc README for proofreading and handwritten editing. The generated
document uses normal Letter or A4 pages rather than one continuously
growing PDF page.

The current version deliberately supports a useful subset of RakuDoc
rather than attempting to be a complete RakuDoc publishing system.

PDF pages use the standard PDF core fonts:

=item Times-Roman for normal body text

=item Helvetica Bold for headings

=item Courier for code

=item Helvetica for page footers

Each page has a centered footer in the form:

=begin code
Page M of N
=end code

where C<M> is the current page number and C<N> is the total number of
pages.

=head1 COMMAND LINE

The distribution installs the C<rakudoc2pdf> program.

Execute the program without an input file, or use C<--help> or C<-h>,
to see its usage information:

=begin code

Usage:
rakudoc2pdf INPUT.rakudoc [--output=FILE.pdf] [--media=Letter|A4] [--type=generic|module-readme]

Examples:
rakudoc2pdf README.rakudoc
rakudoc2pdf README.rakudoc --output=README.pdf
rakudoc2pdf README.rakudoc --media=A4
rakudoc2pdf README.rakudoc --type=module-readme

=end code

Only one input file may be specified.

=head1 OPTIONS

=head2 C<--output=FILE.pdf>

Specifies the output PDF file.

For example:

=begin code

rakudoc2pdf README.rakudoc --output=draft.pdf

=end code

If C<--output> is omitted, the output file is created in the
current working directory using the input file's basename, with the
C<.rakudoc> suffix replaced by C<.pdf>.

Thus:

=begin code

docs/README.rakudoc

=end code

produces:

=begin code

./README.pdf

=end code

=head2 C<--media=Letter|A4>

Selects the PDF page media.

The supported values are:

=item C<Letter>

=item C<A4>

The default is C<Letter>.

For example:

=begin code

rakudoc2pdf README.rakudoc --media=A4
rakudoc2pdf README.rakudoc --type=module-readme

=end code

=head2 C<--type=generic|module-readme>

Selects the document type used for validation before the PDF is generated.

The supported values are:

=item C<generic>

Performs no document-type-specific validation. This is the default.

=item C<module-readme>

Validates conventions for a Raku module README. The current validation checks
for duplicate C<=TITLE> and C<=SUBTITLE> directives, a C<=SUBTITLE> that
occurs before C<=TITLE>, and a missing C<=head1 NAME> section.

For example:

=begin code

rakudoc2pdf README.rakudoc --type=module-readme

=end code

If document-type validation fails, the PDF is not generated and the reported
issues are written as an error.

=head2 C<--help>, C<-h>

Displays command-line usage information and exits.

=head1 LIBRARY INTERFACE

The distribution exports the C<rakudoc-to-pdf> routine:

=begin code :lang<raku>

use App::RakuDocToPDF;

my IO::Path $pdf = rakudoc-to-pdf(
    'README.rakudoc',
    :output<README.pdf>,
    :media<Letter>,
    :type<module-readme>,
);

=end code

The C<:output> argument is optional. If it is omitted, the output filename
is derived from the input file's basename and is written in the current
working directory.

The C<:media> argument is also optional and defaults to C<Letter>.

The C<:type> argument is optional and defaults to C<generic>. The supported
values are C<generic> and C<module-readme>. Document-type validation is
performed before layout and PDF generation.

The routine returns the C<IO::Path> of the generated PDF file.

=head1 SUPPORTED RAKUDOC

The current version is intended primarily for ordinary module README files.
It will eventually handle other uses such as

=item creating PDF slide decks

=item multi-purpose documents based on enhanced Rakudoc with features such as

=item2 controlled page breaks

=item2 embeddable figures of various types

It currently recognizes and renders:

=item headings written with C<=headN>

=item ordinary paragraphs

=item C<=item> entries

=item C<=code> lines

=item C<=begin code> and C<=end code> blocks

=item the visible contents of common C<B<>>, C<I<>>, and C<C<>> inline formatting codes

=item the visible label of a labeled C<L<>> link

Text is wrapped to the available page width, and new PDF pages are created as needed.

=head1 REPRODUCIBLE OUTPUT

The renderer is designed so that identical RakuDoc input and identical
rendering options produce identical PDF bytes. Volatile PDF metadata, such
as the current creation time, is not written. The PDF file identifier is
derived deterministically from the input text and media choice.

This makes generated README drafts suitable for checksum comparison and
prevents a PDF from appearing to change when its rendered content has not.

=head1 LIMITATIONS

This is an intentionally lightweight renderer. It does not yet implement the complete RakuDoc specification.

In particular:

=item Inline C<B<>>, C<I<>>, and C<C<>> markup is currently rendered as ordinary visible text rather than with bold, italic, or code styling.

=item Labeled links retain their visible label, but the generated PDF does not currently create active PDF hyperlinks.

=item Unsupported RakuDoc block directives are currently ignored.

=item Richer RakuDoc features such as tables and images are not currently rendered.

=item The current output is intended primarily as a clean printable draft for proofreading rather than as publication-quality typesetting.

These limitations may be reduced in later releases while retaining the simple draft-generation use case.

